From 8a0da1b4237cbcfd46a64c9b8127b8745193bbb0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: OpenWCH Phase 6 <phase6@openwch.local>
Date: Sun, 16 Aug 2026 19:13:12 +0900
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] RISC-V: decode the WCH XW compressed accesses under
 objdump -M xw

The XW encodings the assembler now emits had no way back.  objdump has no
switch for them, so an object full of XW halfwords still disassembles
through the double-precision slots those halfwords occupy:

  $ objdump -d xwall.o             0: 2188  fld     fa0,0(a1)
  $ objdump -d -M xw xwall.o       0: 2188  lbu     a0,0(a1)
  $ objdump -d -M xw,no-aliases    0: 2188  c.lbu   a0,0(a1)

The vendor objdump adds exactly one -M value and nothing else.  Its -M face
is not table driven; it is a three-way strcmp chain whose third arm is
"xw", and every other name still falls through to the stock "unrecognized
disassembler option" error.  So "XW" and "Xw" are rejected, and a bad name
in a comma list does not stop the remaining names from taking effect
(tmp/toolchain_8.2.0/evidence/s1/optsweep/optsweep.md section 2.4, measured
in tmp/toolchain_8.2.0/evidence/s1/optsweep/raw/probe/B-M-*).  This adds the
same third arm.  The vendor build compares the three bytes inline and
therefore carries no "xw" string literal, which is a code generation
artefact, not behaviour, and is not reproduced.

The option sets the wch_rvc_extension flag that the XW opcode entries
already read through match_with_wch_rvc_extension, which is where this
meets the assembler side of the same gate: gas raises that flag from
-march, the XW entries and the c.f{ld,sd}{,sp} entries hang off the two
complementary matchers, and the default disassembler options clear it.
Nothing but -M xw changes what objdump prints, so the default output stays
what it was -- an XW architecture attribute alone still decodes these
halfwords as c.fld, exactly as the vendor objdump does.  Because the two
matchers are complementary, at most one family can match a given halfword
for either value of the flag, so table order does not enter into it and the
XW rows stay where the assembler patch put them.

The four XW address operands also needed printers.  They existed only on
the assembler side because nothing had ever printed one, and without them
-M xw would drop the offset.

--help gains the vendor's own two lines, byte for byte, including the seven
tabs and the trailing " ." on the continuation line
(tmp/toolchain_8.2.0/evidence/s1/probes/raw/06-hidden-options/
help-objdump.stdout lines 78-79).

gas/testsuite: 5 new tests, every expected line taken from a vendor objdump
run over the same object (tmp/toolchain_8.2.0/evidence/s3/d3/spec-probe):
xw-dis, xw-dis-no-aliases, xw-dis-numeric and xw-dis-off over the existing
xw-compressed.s, plus xw-dis-imm over xw-imm.s.  The last one is what
separates the four operand printers: at offset zero all four print 0.
